{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TpPfCTgATdVxw4wcrVtuVY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gap hunt: A01 — Distinct agent identity\n2\t\n3\tA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\n4\tcheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\n5\tlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n6\t\n7\tFor each check, decide:\n8\t- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n9\t  value never used, an exception that continues, a filter that lets something through).\n10\t  Quote that line;\n11\t- **holds**: you found the code that makes it true in every case;\n12\t- **unclear**: you can't tell from this repo.\n13\t\n14\t**The requirement for A01:** Give the agent its own non-human identity. Keep it separate from the user who launched it and from unrelated agents. Show that permissions and revocation belong to that identity, even when a user starts the run.\n15\t\n16\tIf a check fails, also copy the words from this requirement that the code breaks into\n17\t`violates`. Then write your answer (format at the end).\n18\t\n19\t\n20\t## A01.1\n21\t\n22\t**Check:** The agent runs under its own workload principal (service account, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID) that no other service or unrelated agent uses.\n23\t\n24\t**How to verify:** 1) Find the agent's identity: pod `serviceAccountName`, IAM role, SPIFFE ID, or agent ID in IaC or deploy config. 2) Search other deployments, services, and agents in the repo for the same name or role. 3) Answer no if it is shared, `default`, the main backend's identity, or only a string label with no credential. Answer unknown if identity is assigned outside this repo.\n25\t\n26\t**Reviewer's evidence:** deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml:19 — \"serviceAccountName: procurement-agent\"\n27\t\n28\t**Reviewer's note:** The pod runs as a dedicated procurement-agent service account\n29\t\n30\t`deploy/k8s/deployment.yaml` lines 7–31 (cited lines marked >):\n31\t```\n32\t   7      app: procurement-agent\n33\t   8      owner: team-procurement-platform\n34\t   9  spec:\n35\t  10    replicas: 3\n36\t  11    selector:\n37\t  12      matchLabels:\n38\t  13        app: procurement-agent\n39\t  14    template:\n40\t  15      metadata:\n41\t  16        labels:\n42\t  17          app: procurement-agent\n43\t  18      spec:\n44\t  19>       serviceAccountName: procurement-agent\n45\t  20        automountServiceAccountToken: false\n46\t  21        hostUsers: false\n47\t  22        enableServiceLinks: false\n48\t  23        securityContext:\n49\t  24          runAsNonRoot: true\n50\t  25          runAsUser: 65532\n51\t  26          runAsGroup: 65532\n52\t  27          fsGroup: 65532\n53\t  28          seccompProfile:\n54\t  29            type: RuntimeDefault\n55\t  30        containers:\n56\t  31          - name: agent\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\t## A01.2\n60\t\n61\t**Check:** Tool and API clients get credentials from the agent's own principal, not from the launching user's forwarded token or session cookie.\n62\t\n63\t**How to verify:** 1) Find every place tool and API clients get a token or key. 2) Trace where each comes from: workload identity or token exchange, versus the incoming request's `Authorization` header or session cookie. 3) Check every client, not just the main one. 4) Answer no if any client forwards the user's token or cookie as-is, or uses a shared static key.\n64\t\n65\t**Reviewer's evidence:** app/identity.py:104 — \"\"subject_token\": self._workload_token(),\"\n66\t\n67\t**Reviewer's note:** All clients take audience-bound tokens exchanged from the workload token\n68\t\n69\t`app/identity.py` lines 92–116 (cited lines marked >):\n70\t```\n71\t  92          return Path(self.settings.workload_token_path).read_text().strip()\n72\t  93  \n73\t  94      async def token_for(self, audience: str, scope: str, ident: RunIdentity) -> str:\n74\t  95          key = (audience, scope, ident.tenant)\n75\t  96          cached = self._cache.get(key)\n76\t  97          if cached and cached.expires_at - 30 > time.time():\n77\t  98              return cached.value\n78\t  99          async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=5.0) as client:\n79\t 100              resp = await client.post(\n80\t 101                  self.settings.sts_url,\n81\t 102                  data={\n82\t 103                      \"grant_type\": \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\",\n83\t 104>                     \"subject_token\": self._workload_token(),\n84\t 105                      \"subject_token_type\": \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt\",\n85\t 106                      \"audience\": audience,\n86\t 107                      \"scope\": scope,\n87\t 108                      \"resource\": f\"urn:bu:{ident.tenant}\",\n88\t 109                      \"actor_token\": ident.delegated_subject,\n89\t 110                      \"actor_token_type\": \"urn:example:params:subject-id\",\n90\t 111                  },\n91\t 112              )\n92\t 113              resp.raise_for_status()\n93\t 114              body = resp.json()\n94\t 115          token = _CachedToken(body[\"access_token\"], time.time() + int(body[\"expires_in\"]))\n95\t 116          self._cache[key] = token\n96\t```\n97\t\n98\t## A01.3\n99\t\n100\t**Check:** When a user starts a run, the user is carried as a delegated subject (for example a token exchange with separate act and sub), while the agent keeps its own principal.\n101\t\n102\t**How to verify:** 1) Find the code that starts a run for a user. 2) Look for token exchange (RFC 8693, `subject_token`, `actor_token`, `act`/`sub` claims) or a separate delegated-subject field. 3) Check the agent's own principal is still the actor. 4) Answer no if the user's token is used directly or the user replaces the agent identity. Answer unknown if the exchange happens in an outside identity service.\n103\t\n104\t**Reviewer's evidence:** app/identity.py:109 — \"\"actor_token\": ident.delegated_subject,\"\n105\t\n106\t**Reviewer's note:** The launching user is carried as a delegated subject in the exchange\n107\t\n108\t`app/identity.py` lines 97–117 (cited lines marked >):\n109\t```\n110\t  97          if cached and cached.expires_at - 30 > time.time():\n111\t  98              return cached.value\n112\t  99          async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=5.0) as client:\n113\t 100              resp = await client.post(\n114\t 101                  self.settings.sts_url,\n115\t 102                  data={\n116\t 103                      \"grant_type\": \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\",\n117\t 104                      \"subject_token\": self._workload_token(),\n118\t 105                      \"subject_token_type\": \"urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt\",\n119\t 106                      \"audience\": audience,\n120\t 107                      \"scope\": scope,\n121\t 108                      \"resource\": f\"urn:bu:{ident.tenant}\",\n122\t 109>                     \"actor_token\": ident.delegated_subject,\n123\t 110                      \"actor_token_type\": \"urn:example:params:subject-id\",\n124\t 111                  },\n125\t 112              )\n126\t 113              resp.raise_for_status()\n127\t 114              body = resp.json()\n128\t 115          token = _CachedToken(body[\"access_token\"], time.time() + int(body[\"expires_in\"]))\n129\t 116          self._cache[key] = token\n130\t 117          return token.value\n131\t```\n132\t\n133\t## A01.4\n134\t\n135\t**Check:** Permissions are bound to the agent principal in IaC or RBAC, so revoking that principal alone removes the agent's access without disabling the user.\n136\t\n137\t**How to verify:** 1) Find IAM policies, RoleBindings, or grants in Terraform, Helm, or Kubernetes YAML. 2) Check they bind to the agent principal, not to a user, group, or shared role. 3) Check that removing that binding alone would cut the agent's access. 4) Answer no if access depends on the user's grants or a shared credential. Answer unknown if permissions are not defined in this repo.\n138\t\n139\t**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n140\t\n141\t**Reviewer's note:** RBAC, IAM and STS policy that bind permissions to the principal are outside this repo\n142\t\n143\t(no evidence cited)\n144\t\n145\t## Your answer\n146\t\n147\tWrite this JSON to A01.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"A01\"):\n148\t```json\n149\t{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n150\t \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"A01.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \"evidence\": \"path:line\", \"quote\": \"exact code\", \"note\": \"why\"}],\n151\t \"note\": \"one sentence\"}\n152\t```\n153\tVerdict is **failed** if any check fails, **unclear** if none fails but any is unclear, otherwise **upheld**. A failed check needs the quoted line that makes it false: `evidence` is path:line, and `quote` is the exact text of that line. To cite two places, list both refs in `evidence` (`a.py:12, b.yaml:40`) and put each line's text on its own line in `quote`. If the check fails, add `\"violates\": [\"words copied exactly from the requirement\"]`.\n154\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8de8610d-fd25-42b6-a05e-9f8d9f848411","uuid":"d981f2c0-d18b-45c8-a667-6263abf7c1ec","timestamp":"2026-10-02T14:33:31.947Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-challenger-hfr2ta7q/sheet.md","content":"# Gap hunt: A01 — Distinct agent identity\n\nA reviewer couldn't decide whether this item is met. **Your job is to find out whether any\ncheck below is false in this codebase.** The reviewer's partial answers and the code they\nlooked at are shown. Read the code, and open the repo files the checks point to.\n\nFor each check, decide:\n- **fails**: you found the line that makes it false (a missing control, a bypass, a\n  value never used, an exc